# Chronolgy error

Atlantic Community Bank is filing this SAR on customer Marcus Bennett, age 44, of Raleigh, North Carolina, and Bennett Creative Solutions LLC regarding suspected receipt and movement of business email compromise or other fraud proceeds. Bennett is the sole owner of Bennett Creative Solutions LLC, which maintains business checking account 88241760. The account was opened on February 12, 2024.

At account opening, Bennett described Bennett Creative Solutions LLC as a marketing and graphic design business serving small and midsized companies in North Carolina. Bennett reported expected monthly revenue of approximately $20,000 to $30,000, primarily through ACH payments and checks from recurring commercial clients. Historical account activity from February 2024 through June 2026 was generally consistent with that profile. The account received payments from approximately 15 recurring customers and showed routine outgoing payments for payroll, software subscriptions, office expenses, advertising services, taxes, and transfers to Bennett’s personal account.

Between July 7, 2026, and August 12, 2026, Bennett Creative Solutions LLC received eight incoming domestic wire transfers totaling $236,050 from eight businesses that had not previously transacted with Bennett or his company. Individual wires ranged from $22,600 to $35,900.

The incoming wires were as follows:

July 7: $28,400 from Westbridge Construction Inc. of Columbus, Ohio.
July 13: $31,750 from Apex Dental Equipment LLC of Tampa, Florida.
July 20: $26,400 from Greenline Property Management Inc. of Richmond, Virginia.
July 27: $35,900 from Monarch Industrial Supply LLC of Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.
August 1: $22,600 from Bellweather Legal Services PC of Chicago, Illinois.
August 5: $29,850 from Summit Office Products Inc. of Nashville, Tennessee.
August 9: $33,400 from Horizon Mechanical Services LLC of Baltimore, Maryland.
August 12: $27,750 from Lakeside Medical Group LLC of Jacksonville, Florida.

Wire information described the payments using terms including “invoice payment,” “professional services,” “consulting,” and “vendor payment.” Review of Bennett Creative Solutions LLC’s prior transaction history identified no previous payments from the eight senders. Bank records, customer information, and open-source research identified no apparent ownership relationship between Bennett and the sending businesses.

Between July 8 and August 13, 2026, Bennett initiated four outgoing international wire transfers totaling $222,050. The wires were initiated through Atlantic Community Bank’s online banking platform from IP addresses resolving to the Raleigh, North Carolina area and were sent to three commercial beneficiaries.

On July 8, Bennett sent $54,900 to Pacific Star Electronics Ltd. at a financial institution in Hong Kong. On July 21, Bennett sent $61,500 to the same beneficiary. On August 2, Bennett sent $47,250 to Meridian Global Trading FZE at a financial institution in the United Arab Emirates. On August 13, Bennett sent $58,400 to Eastern Crown Technology Ltd. at a financial institution in Hong Kong.

The four outgoing international wires represented approximately 94 percent of the incoming wire activity described above. Bennett Creative Solutions LLC had no history of international wire activity during the preceding 29 months.

The timing of the transactions showed rapid movement of funds following receipt. For example, Bennett received $28,400 from Westbridge Construction Inc. and $31,750 from Apex Dental Equipment LLC on July 7 and July 13, respectively, and subsequently sent $54,900 to Pacific Star Electronics Ltd. on July 8. Bennett received $26,400 from Greenline Property Management Inc. on July 20 and sent $61,500 to Pacific Star Electronics Ltd. the following day, using that incoming payment together with funds already present in the account.

On August 4, 2026, Atlantic Community Bank received a wire recall request from the financial institution that originated the July 13 $31,750 payment from Apex Dental Equipment LLC. The sending institution advised that its customer reported being deceived by fraudulent payment instructions received through a compromised business email account. According to the recall request, Apex Dental Equipment LLC intended to pay an existing vendor but was provided altered wiring instructions directing payment to Bennett Creative Solutions LLC.

Atlantic Community Bank returned $4,360 remaining from the disputed funds. The remainder had already been transferred from the account.

On August 7, 2026, Atlantic Community Bank received a separate fraud inquiry concerning the July 27 $35,900 wire from Monarch Industrial Supply LLC. The originating financial institution advised that Monarch reported receiving an altered vendor invoice directing payment to Bennett Creative Solutions LLC instead of its intended supplier. The sending institution requested return of any remaining funds associated with the transaction.

Atlantic Community Bank has not received fraud notifications concerning the other six incoming wires and has not independently established that those transactions originated from fraudulent activity.

On August 8, 2026, Atlantic Community Bank contacted Bennett regarding the incoming wires and his recent international activity. Bennett stated that he had entered into an arrangement with an individual he identified as “Daniel Wu,” whom he met through an online business networking forum in June 2026. Bennett stated that Wu represented an overseas electronics sourcing company and asked Bennett to receive payments from U.S. customers because the overseas company was experiencing difficulty receiving domestic payments directly.

Bennett stated that he was instructed to retain approximately 5 percent of the funds received and wire the remainder to overseas suppliers identified by Wu. Bennett said he believed the arrangement was a legitimate business opportunity and that his role was limited to receiving and forwarding payments.

Bennett provided copies of email communications with an individual using the address daniel.wu@globalprocuremail.com. The emails included instructions identifying incoming payment amounts and directing Bennett where to send subsequent international wires. The communications did not include a formal contract between Bennett Creative Solutions LLC and Wu or any of the overseas beneficiary companies.

Bennett also provided three invoices bearing the names Pacific Star Electronics Ltd., Meridian Global Trading FZE, and Eastern Crown Technology Ltd. The invoices described purchases of computer hardware and consumer electronics. None identified Bennett Creative Solutions LLC as the purchaser or seller of the goods, and Bennett did not provide shipping documentation, customer orders, or records showing that Bennett Creative Solutions LLC had provided marketing or graphic design services to any of the eight businesses sending funds.

Open-source research identified Bennett Creative Solutions LLC as an active North Carolina company associated with marketing and graphic design. Pacific Star Electronics Ltd., Meridian Global Trading FZE, and Eastern Crown Technology Ltd. appeared in publicly available corporate or commercial records, but Atlantic Community Bank did not identify information establishing a prior relationship between those entities and Bennett or his company. No sanctions matches were identified for Bennett, Bennett Creative Solutions LLC, or the named overseas beneficiaries.

Atlantic Community Bank considers the activity suspicious because Bennett Creative Solutions LLC received substantial payments from multiple previously unrelated businesses, two of which were subsequently reported by originating financial institutions as having resulted from altered or fraudulent payment instructions. Bennett rapidly transferred most of the incoming funds to overseas beneficiaries despite having no prior international wire activity and despite the activity being inconsistent with his company’s stated marketing and graphic design business.

The arrangement described by Bennett, including receiving third-party commercial payments, retaining a percentage, and forwarding the remaining funds at the direction of an individual he recently met online, is consistent with possible money mule activity and movement of suspected fraud proceeds. The two reported fraud incidents further increase the bank’s concern that the account may be receiving proceeds of business email compromise schemes. Atlantic Community Bank has not established that Bennett knowingly participated in the underlying fraud or that all incoming payments during the review period were fraudulent.

Atlantic Community Bank restricted Bennett Creative Solutions LLC from initiating additional outgoing wires on August 14, 2026 and placed the account under enhanced review. The account remains open. Account statements, incoming and outgoing wire records, fraud recall requests, customer communications, invoices provided by Bennett, online banking records, customer due diligence information, and related investigative records are available upon request.
